1
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:11,306
Episode one ended with a picture of four lands joined by seven bridges,

2
00:00:11,306 --> 00:00:14,448
and now we give its parts their proper names.

3
00:00:14,448 --> 00:00:20,992
So the four lands are the vertices, and together they form one set, which we call V.

4
00:00:20,992 --> 00:00:27,938
And the seven bridges are the edges, and together they form a second set, which we call E.

5
00:00:28,316 --> 00:00:32,496
Look closely at E, because two of its pairs appear twice,

6
00:00:32,496 --> 00:00:36,750
since the city has two bridges between the same two lands.

7
00:00:36,750 --> 00:00:44,190
So E here is a multiset, which means a set in which an element may appear several times.

8
00:00:44,550 --> 00:00:49,626
In this course we usually forbid that, so from now on E is a plain set,

9
00:00:49,626 --> 00:00:53,344
and the second copy of each doubled pair disappears.

10
00:00:53,344 --> 00:00:59,992
Then between any two vertices there is either no edge or exactly one edge.

11
00:01:01,650 --> 00:01:08,648
So far every bridge could be crossed in both directions, but some relationships go only one way,

12
00:01:08,648 --> 00:01:10,252
like a one way street.

13
00:01:10,252 --> 00:01:16,718
So a directed graph models this with arrows, and its edges are ordered pairs.

14
00:01:16,918 --> 00:01:25,352
Here the vertex called one has an edge to two, to three and to four, so E holds exactly three ordered pairs.

15
00:01:25,352 --> 00:01:32,850
That makes E a subset of V times V, the set of all ordered pairs of vertices.

16
00:01:33,216 --> 00:01:40,994
Order matters in a directed graph, so the ordered pair one, two is an edge, while the ordered pair two,

17
00:01:40,994 --> 00:01:41,824
one is not.

18
00:01:41,824 --> 00:01:47,830
And you can follow the arrow from one to two, but nothing lets you come back along it.

19
00:01:48,183 --> 00:01:54,775
On the other hand, when every edge works in both directions, we call the graph undirected,

20
00:01:54,775 --> 00:01:57,265
like a network of two way streets.

21
00:01:57,265 --> 00:02:05,023
And then we drop the arrowheads and write each edge as a set with two members, instead of an ordered pair.

22
00:02:05,383 --> 00:02:15,235
In both cases a graph is a pair, G is the ordered pair V, E, where V is the vertex set and E is the edge set.

23
00:02:15,235 --> 00:02:22,835
So the undirected graph on the right has five vertices and five edges, and it is the one we use next.

24
00:02:23,716 --> 00:02:29,036
Take the undirected graph on the right and look at the vertex numbered three.

25
00:02:29,036 --> 00:02:35,539
So its three edges are incident on it, and the vertices at their other ends are its neighbours,

26
00:02:35,539 --> 00:02:37,456
which we also call adjacent.

27
00:02:37,816 --> 00:02:44,950
The degree of a vertex is the number of edges incident on it, so the vertex three has degree three.

28
00:02:44,950 --> 00:02:51,322
And counting the same way, the vertices one, two and four all have degree two,

29
00:02:51,322 --> 00:02:54,344
while the vertex five has degree one.

30
00:02:54,716 --> 00:03:00,612
Now add a lonely sixth vertex with no edges at all, and its degree is zero.

31
00:03:00,612 --> 00:03:06,838
Such a vertex is called isolated, because no edge connects it to the rest of the graph.

32
00:03:06,838 --> 00:03:14,830
And we agree that no edge may start and end at the same vertex, so our graphs have no self loops.

33
00:03:15,183 --> 00:03:22,073
A directed graph has two kinds of degree, because an arrow can come into a vertex or leave it.

34
00:03:22,073 --> 00:03:26,559
And the in-degree of a vertex counts the edges arriving at it,

35
00:03:26,559 --> 00:03:30,105
while the out-degree counts the edges leaving it.

36
00:03:30,305 --> 00:03:36,647
So in the graph on the left, vertex one has out-degree three and in-degree zero.

37
00:03:36,647 --> 00:03:42,308
And each of the other three vertices has in-degree one and out-degree zero,

38
00:03:42,308 --> 00:03:45,177
since every arrow ends at one of them.

39
00:03:46,850 --> 00:03:54,272
Let us explore a small neighbourhood, where each vertex is a house and each edge is a direct road.

40
00:03:54,272 --> 00:04:02,946
So the roads join houses one and two, one and three, one and four, two and three, and three and four.

41
00:04:03,316 --> 00:04:08,654
Suppose we want to drive from house two to house four, which is a job for a path.

42
00:04:08,654 --> 00:04:16,656
A path is a sequence of edges from the first house to the last, and here it never visits a house twice.

43
00:04:16,856 --> 00:04:25,444
So two, one, four is a shortest path, with two edges, because no single road joins houses two and four.

44
00:04:25,444 --> 00:04:34,022
And the longest path that repeats no house is two, three, one, four, which has three edges.

45
00:04:34,383 --> 00:04:41,777
A cycle is a path that returns to where it began, so we add one edge from the last house back to the first.

46
00:04:41,777 --> 00:04:51,433
For example, one, two, three and back to one is a cycle, and so is one, three, four and back to one.

47
00:04:51,783 --> 00:04:58,129
Now suppose we only want a leisurely stroll, going from two to one, back to two,

48
00:04:58,129 --> 00:05:00,429
then to three and on to four.

49
00:05:00,429 --> 00:05:08,887
That sequence of edges is a walk, and it may repeat houses and even edges, which a path never does.

50
00:05:09,087 --> 00:05:17,963
And a tour is a walk that ends where it started, so two, one, three, four, one, two is a tour.

51
00:05:18,316 --> 00:05:25,830
To keep the four words apart, remember that a path has no repeated vertices and a tour is closed.

52
00:05:25,830 --> 00:05:33,526
A cycle is both, apart from the start and end that coincide, and a walk has no rule at all.

53
00:05:34,383 --> 00:05:36,594
Now we can define connectivity,

54
00:05:36,594 --> 00:05:42,159
and a graph is connected if there is a path between any two distinct vertices.

55
00:05:42,159 --> 00:05:44,705
So our neighbourhood is connected,

56
00:05:44,705 --> 00:05:50,847
since from any house we can drive to any other house along some sequence of roads.

57
00:05:51,216 --> 00:05:53,850
Now look at a network of seven houses,

58
00:05:53,850 --> 00:05:59,602
where no road joins the left triangle to the lonely house or to the right triangle.

59
00:05:59,602 --> 00:06:06,380
So the network is not connected, and it falls apart into exactly three connected components.

60
00:06:06,380 --> 00:06:16,825
Those components are the sets V one, V two and V three, holding houses one, two, three, then four alone,

61
00:06:16,825 --> 00:06:19,034
then five, six, seven.

62
00:06:19,416 --> 00:06:23,340
With these words we can state Euler's theorem exactly,

63
00:06:23,340 --> 00:06:27,628
since an Eulerian walk is a walk that uses every edge once.

64
00:06:27,628 --> 00:06:32,126
And an Eulerian tour is one of these walks that ends where it started.

65
00:06:32,126 --> 00:06:40,266
The theorem says that an undirected graph has an Eulerian tour exactly when every degree is even and the

66
00:06:40,266 --> 00:06:41,753
graph is connected,

67
00:06:41,753 --> 00:06:43,866
ignoring isolated vertices.

68
00:06:44,216 --> 00:06:50,196
In this network every degree is even, and yet no Eulerian tour exists.

69
00:06:50,196 --> 00:06:56,796
So a tour that starts in the left triangle can never reach the right triangle, since no road joins them.

70
00:06:56,796 --> 00:07:03,886
That is why Euler's theorem needs both conditions, the even degrees and the connectedness.

